  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe ~1035mb high pressure system is centered over northeastern SD/ND \nborder as of 18Z. This high will continue to drift off towards the \nsoutheast today and tonight with the center of the high over IA by \n12Z Tuesday. Mid to high clouds will continue to move in from the \nwest through the rest of this afternoon and evening (per shortwave \naloft) and overspreading the CWA by this evening with with lower \nclouds moving in from the southwest tonight and overnight. \nOtherwise, the forecast remains overall dry through tonight. Tuesday \nmorning through the midday, a shortwave trough will be moving in \nfrom the west and centered over the region by 18Z with the upper \nlevel low (and its positive tilted trough) spinning over southern \nSaskatchewan. By 00Z Wednesday, the center of the upper low is \nforecast to be positioned over northern ND where it will continue to \ntrack southeastward, with the center over northeastern SD/ND border \nby 06Z Wednesday. This low will be the driver for light snow. \nAdd in a southwesterly flow LLJ strengthening over the James \nValley and eastward, with speeds of 20-25kts, adds additional \nlift near the surface combining with the lift aloft and\nmoisture.\n\nKABR RAP soundings by midday-afternoon Tuesday indicate the profile \nbecoming saturated to about 900mb with very shallow dry air at the \nsurface (as we are on the backside of this high). So snow will start \nout light or flurries. As the snow falls, web bulb temp become \nsaturated which is when we expect the \"bulk\" of the snowfall \n(Tuesday afternoon through late Tuesday night). Same thing \noccurs at KATY but delayed by a couple hours per track of \nlow/precip. No lack of lift within the DGZ and a bit below. So \nsnow ratios will most likely range from 8:1-10:1 given temps at \nthe surface are going to be right around freezing to a couple \ndegrees above freezing during the day and lows in the 20s.\n\nNBM has been really struggling on the pops and QPF as they have been \nway too low over the past couple of days with HRRR almost being to \naggressive on pops and QPF. Model guidance all agree that the light \nsnow will mainly fall James Valley and eastward with the bulk of it \nCoteau and eastward which gives us high confidence on location. \nHowever, exact snowfall amounts vary by a few tenths here and there \nbetween the models. REFS prob of 0.1\" or higher is at 20-50%, \nHowever compared to global models ENS and GEPS indicate this \nprobability above 80% (more saturation and lift) while GEFS is only \nat 40% with sounding profiles indicating less saturation overall \nand forcing. As mentioned, all agree on the highest QPF Coteau and \neastward (especially far east central SD). So in collaboration with \nsurrounding offices, bumped up the pops and QPF with a blend of \nConshort/HRRR/RRFS to up what the NBM is lacking to show. \nProbability of snow of an inch or more is up to 15% and thats \nover far east central SD. With sidewalks/roads on the warm side,\nnot expected much in the way of accumulation with most of it on\ngrassy surfaces, cars, etc. Overall travel impacts are expected\nto be low as winds will gust to 25 during the afternoon and 20 \nmph in the evening James Valley and eastward ad no snow on the\nground to blow. Nothing indicated in the WSSI for winter \nimpacts. With temps warming to above freezing the next day, any \nsnow left over will melt. Otherwise dry weather expected for the\nrest of Wednesday into Thanksgiving for ongoing holiday travel.\nFriday there could be a slight potential for far NESD/MN to be \nclipped by some rain as a system will track mainly over \nND/MN/Canada. As of now pops are less than 20% for this area. \nLow confidence on setup this far out.",
